on show  them the things a human would get to see  if they were measuring the simulation  and then the action in this case is that  the agent gets to decide how to  rearrange the animals within the kennels  to minimize the spread of the infection  or where to place new animals when they  come into the system so there are many  levels of difficulty in in this problem  you can kind of define it in terms of  what arbitrary variables you're allowing  to change the the most general version  of this would be of an arbitrary graph  any graph and an arbitrary point in time  in the the progression of the outbreak  within your simulation and an arbitrary  state meaning any rearrangement of the  animals  then the agent should be able to make an  optimal choice no matter what those  different configurations are I did make  the choice like I said to make it all  planar graphs you could also lift that  restriction maybe there are stacked  candles when you look at it in run a  bunch of simulations letting all these  things vary you if you start the  simulation from zero animals which is  not common but also not not entirely  uncommon because oftentimes they'll spin  up these intake areas when they start to  notice disease in their population so it  sort of starts from zero you see this  this sort of common disease saturation  curve that'll happen where it  asymptotically approaches a particular  infection rate and that infection rate  just holds sort of indefinitely in the  future and so that's really what we want  to measure is how well you're doing with  that asymptotic infection rate and you  can you can sort of look over time at  what this looks like it is it unfolds  you can also restrict the problem a bit  say okay we're we're really a we maybe  can retrain the algorithm every time  there is a new shelter that has an  outbreak on their graph of whatever  their shelter is that makes it a little  bit easier we don't have to train on any  graph we can restrict it even further  and say well maybe we can retrain for  halfway through this curve so we're  assuming we're gonna get there after  four days we don't have to train for  this beginning part where we're spinning  up the system they'll take our advice  they'll start the thing and then we'll  come in and start giving them a strategy  on day five something like that which  also makes the problem easier and then  finally the problem that I ended up  attempting to solve is take all those  restrictions and then add on to it the  restriction that we're gonna take a  literal single point in time and a  single situation within the graph which  is about as restricted to cases you can  have that's a very it's it's borderline  not useful to be able to do that um  you'd have to retrain the agent  basically every day which is you know  not not ideal but when you do this it  actually turns out to be equivalent to  sorting a list of values where you the  values are probability distributions  that you don't know so you're sampling  the values which is kind of an  interesting math observation so you in  order to train this thing you have to  have a huge amount of observations  it's a stochastic problem so that makes  it even harder it's very sensitive to  those initial conditions which makes it  a somewhat chaotic system I hadn't  proven that and then there are a lot of  edge cases that we know about for  instance if the graph configuration has  all unconnected  bizzy's doesn't spread so we know about  those and there's probably a bunch of  education we don't know about so this  low-level problem is still really hard  and like I said is equivalent to sorting  sort of a bunch of unknown values which  is a solvable problem but not easy to  solve and so I took my shot at solving  that version of the problem after  realizing I wasn't gonna solve any of  those top ones and actually was able to  train an agent that was able to tie the  best human strategy which in this  particular case I eliminated the room  locking because it's sort of cheating  you're allowing yourself to not take in  animals which is not the reality of the  situation and so trained against the  snake versus the random and was able to  tie the the trick in training this thing  is a lot of times it likes to do nothing  over doing something seemingly that  works better and I think the reason is  because it's so easy to do something  that makes it worse because of those  properties we talked about earlier which  is that if you change the adjacency of  two animals then you're more likely to  spread the disease more and so the  actual choices you make have to be very  careful and verified - so training  something like this is not easy and I  think really the limitations here are a  lot more computational power I was just  running this on my local computer and  anyone who's done reinforcement learning  knows you benefit a lot from being able  to run it several million times and not  several tens of thousands of times and  then also just sort of a better  understanding of some of those other  sort of onion layered versions of the  problem so a lot more to talk about  there but let's move on cuz I've got ten  minutes to go over one more thing which  is this sort of high-level  problem-solving oriented approach to how  shelters work in general so shelters in  the u.s. they're about 13,000 known  rescues and shelters in the US alone  they serve we think somewhere between  six and eight million animals a year  shelters are really just designed to  serve as many animals as possible as  efficiently as possible and they're  constantly overwhelmed and so many of  them don't know what data to write down  they don't have time to figure it out  they kind of write down everything or  nothing or somewhere in between more or  less at random and they don't really  know the best way of measuring their  success and doing so in a way that when  they develop a strategy that works for a  certain population like we have with  p